<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<conversations>
  <!-- No Load attribute, deliberately. ConversationLoader merges into an existing blueprint only
       when Load == 0, which is what an absent attribute means - vanilla's own Conversations.xml
       declares BaseConversation three separate times and relies on exactly that. "Replace" would
       discard every choice the base carries, the water ritual and [begin trade] included. -->
  <conversation ID="BaseConversation">
    <!-- A <choice> whose parent is <conversation> gets Distribute="Start" assigned automatically
         (ConversationXMLBlueprint, on reading the node), which is how it reaches the start node of
         every conversation in the game. The same mechanism carries vanilla's water ritual at
         Ordinal 980 and [begin trade] at 990.

         Target="TellName" is vanilla's own node, declared in BaseConversation and inherited
         everywhere, so the reply is Freehold's text rather than mine.

         The question pool is one <text> with ~ separators: IConversationElement.Prepare calls
         GetRandomSubstring('~'), so one is drawn per asking. The first six are vanilla's, kept
         verbatim; the rest are this fork's. -->
    <choice ID="Vixy_AskName" Target="TellName" Ordinal="10000">
      <text>
        What is your name?~
        What is your name, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        What may I call you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        What are you called, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        I am =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=. What is your name?~
        I am =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=. What may I call you?~
        How may I address you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        By what name are you known?~
        What name do you carry?~
        What do your people call you?~
        I am =name=. What are you called?
      </text>
      <part Name="Vixy_AskName" />
    </choice>

    <!-- Show someone their own work. #595.

         The issue is titled for a gap that is mostly not there: the item's own `MakersMark` part
         already holds `CrafterName` and already prints *"this dagger bears the mark of Argyve"*,
         and `GenericInventoryRestocker` already stamps hero-merchant stock and raises its value.
         What nothing did was **react** - you could carry a blade back to the person who made it and
         he had nothing to say. That is the only part built here.

         Ordinal 9800 puts it below Vixy_AskName's 10000 and above vanilla's water ritual at 980 and
         [begin trade] at 990, so it reads before the transactional choices without displacing the
         question that opens a conversation.

         The reply is one node for every maker, so its text has to be true of any of them - hence a
         line about the hand rather than about the object. `=subject.refname=` is vanilla's own
         substitution, the same one `TellName` uses. -->
    <choice ID="Vixy_ShowMakersMark" Target="Vixy_MakersMarkAcknowledged" Ordinal="9800">
      <text>
        You made this.~
        I am carrying something of yours.~
        This bears your mark, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=.~
        I have a piece of your work here.~
        Your mark is on something I carry.
      </text>
      <part Name="Vixy_ShowMakersMark" />
    </choice>

    <node ID="Vixy_MakersMarkAcknowledged">
      <text>So it does. I know my own hand.</text>
      <text>That is my mark. I remember the work, if not the day.</text>
      <text>Mine, yes. It has been further than I have.</text>
      <text>I made that. Keep it in better repair than the last one I saw.</text>
      <choice Target="Start">
        <text>It is good work, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=.</text>
        <text>It has served me well.</text>
        <text>I thought you would want to know.</text>
      </choice>
    </node>

    <!-- The recruit's level, on the water ritual's join choice. #592.

         `WaterRitualJoinParty` prices recruitment at Math.Max(50, 200 + (theirLevel - myLevel) * 12)
         and shows only the price, so the number is an encoding of their level *above the floor* and
         a flat 50 below it - which is most late-game recruiting. This puts the level itself beside
         it: [250 reputation] [level 14].

         Load="Add" on a part inside an existing choice appends, and appending is the requirement
         rather than a detail: vanilla's part assigns E.Tag outright, so anything hoping to add to
         that string has to run after it. -->
    <node ID="WaterRitual">
      <choice ID="JoinPartyChoice">
        <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_RecruitLevel" />
      </choice>
    </node>

    <!-- People I shared water with remember me. #753, first checkbox.

         The water ritual is a one-shot menu and then the relationship is over; nobody ever refers
         to it again whatever I go on to do. This is the smallest thing that changes that, and it is
         deliberately only a remark - nothing is asked, nothing changes hands.

         `Vixy_WaterMemory` writes my standing with their people into `WaterRitualRecord.attributes`
         on the day we share water; `Vixy_WaterRemembers` reads it back and gates this choice on the
         `WaterRitualed` property vanilla already sets and nothing else reads.

         Ordinal 9700 puts it below Vixy_ShowMakersMark's 9800 and Vixy_AskName's 10000, and well
         above vanilla's water ritual at 980 and [begin trade] at 990 - it belongs with the things
         you say to somebody, not with the transactions.

         `=Vixy_ritualreport=` is filled by the part rather than by vanilla's substitution table.
         The `~` pool is collapsed by GetRandomSubstring *before* PrepareTextEvent fires, so a part
         cannot pick which line was drawn - but PrepareTextEvent hands over the StringBuilder and
         runs before the `=variable=` pass, so a token planted here can be filled and still go
         through normal processing. That is what keeps all the prose in this file and lets one node
         cover all three directions. Every line must carry the token, and must read correctly with
         "good report", "ill report" and "little" substituted in. -->
    <!-- Notice when I give somebody my name, whoever wrote the words. #753.

         Vanilla has hand-written introductions in twenty-six conversations, seven of them on people
         who can perform a water ritual - Agyra, Une, Miryam, Tzedech, Tikva, Thicksalt, Tammuz.
         Without this, using vanilla's introduction would leave Vixy_RitualGate shut for ever,
         because only Vixy_Introduce set the marker. There was nothing to hook: GenericAskNameOption
         is a template nobody inherits, five of the seven route through GotoID="Name" but Agyra and
         Une do not, and none of them carries a part.

         Register="Listener" is required, not decoration. Conversation events bubble upward, so a
         part here sees every choice taken anywhere - but a conversation-level part registers for
         the Speaker perspective by default, and a choice is mine to speak. Without the override it
         would never see one. -->
    <part Name="Vixy_Introductions" Register="Listener" />

    <!-- Give my name to somebody whose name I already know. #753.

         Vixy_AskName covers the nameless; this covers the named, and the two can never both appear.
         It had to exist before the ritual could be gated on an introduction at all: ElderBob and
         Mehmet both carry GivesRep *and* <xtagGrammar Proper="true" />, so on the population that
         performs the water ritual there was no naming exchange available in either direction.

         Ordinal 9900 puts it above Vixy_ShowMakersMark at 9800 and below Vixy_AskName at 10000 -
         the two naming exchanges sit together at the top, and only ever one of them is visible. -->
    <choice ID="Vixy_Introduce" Target="Vixy_Introduced" Ordinal="9900">
      <text>
        I am =name=.~
        My name is =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=.~
        You may call me =name=.~
        I am called =name=. Now you know it.~
        =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=. That is my name.
      </text>
      <part Name="Vixy_Introduce" />
    </choice>

    <node ID="Vixy_Introduced">
      <text>
        =name=. I will remember it.~
        Then I know you, =name=.~
        =name=. The name is heard.~
        Well met, =name=.~
        I have it, =name=.
      </text>
      <choice Target="Start">Good.~Then we understand each other.~As it should be.</choice>
    </node>

    <!-- Gate vanilla's own water ritual choice, on both sides of the ritual. #753.

         Load="Add" appends to the existing choice rather than replacing it, the same way
         Vixy_RecruitLevel is added to JoinPartyChoice below. Vanilla's choice keeps its text, its
         IfSpeakerHavePart="GivesRep" and its Ordinal of 980; this only answers visibility.

         Before the ritual it hides until a name has passed between us. After it, it hides unless
         the bond can actually deepen - because WaterRitualBegin charges a dram on *every* entry,
         the UseDrams branch sitting outside the first-time check, so a repeat that can do nothing
         still costs water.

         Every uncertain case resolves to visible. Hiding this wrongly would strand a questline;
         showing it wrongly costs a dram. -->
    <choice ID="WaterRitualChoice">
      <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_RitualGate" />
    </choice>

    <choice ID="Vixy_WaterRemembers" Target="Vixy_WaterRemembered" Ordinal="9700">
      <text>
        We shared water once.~
        Water passed between us, you and I.~
        You drank with me, once.~
        There was water between us.~
        Do you remember the water we shared?
      </text>
      <part Name="Vixy_WaterRemembers" />
    </choice>

    <node ID="Vixy_WaterRemembered">
      <text>
        I remember it. Since that day I have heard =Vixy_ritualreport= of you among my people.~
        The water is not forgotten. Since then there has come =Vixy_ritualreport= of you.~
        I remember. My people have spoken =Vixy_ritualreport= of you since.~
        That water still stands between us. Since then there has been =Vixy_ritualreport= of you.~
        I remember the water. And I have heard =Vixy_ritualreport= of you since.
      </text>
      <!-- The same part again, and it has to be here rather than only on the choice above.
           PrepareTextEvent.Send(Element, ...) dispatches to the element whose text it is, so a part
           sitting on the choice never sees the node's text and the token renders raw to the player.
           On the choice it answers IsElementVisibleEvent; on the node it fills the token. -->
      <part Name="Vixy_WaterRemembers" />
      <choice Target="End">Until the next time.</choice>
      <choice Target="Start">There is something else.</choice>
    </node>
  </conversation>

  <!-- Argyve's knickknack quest, both stages. Vanilla's <part Name="GiveArtifact" /> lists every
       artifact I am carrying with no importance check of any kind, so this swaps it for one that
       leaves out the ones I marked. See #570.

       The choice IDs are derived rather than written: a <choice> with no ID takes its Target plus
       the element name, so Target="GiveKnickknack" is addressable as GiveKnickknackChoice and
       Target="Impressive" as ImpressiveChoice. The part node's ID is its Name.

       Remove-then-add rather than Load="Replace": Replace matches on the ID, and a replacement
       node's ID depends on whether the reader saw ID or Name first, which is attribute order and
       not something to depend on. Two unambiguous operations instead of one subtle one. -->
  <conversation ID="Argyve">
    <start ID="StartHasFetch1">
      <choice ID="GiveKnickknackChoice">
        <part ID="GiveArtifact" Load="Remove" />
        <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_GiveArtifact" />
      </choice>
    </start>
    <start ID="StartHasFetch2">
      <choice ID="ImpressiveChoice">
        <part ID="GiveArtifact" Load="Remove" />
        <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_GiveArtifact" />
      </choice>
    </start>
  </conversation>

  <!-- Yla Haj teaches keen. #723.

       One teacher per recipe, so a tinker is a person worth finding rather than one of four
       interchangeable sources. The cost is that killing them removes the recipe from that save.

       Ordinal 9700 puts this below Vixy_ShowMakersMark at 9800 and Vixy_AskName at 10000, and above
       vanilla's water ritual at 980 and [begin trade] at 990 - a thing you ask a tinker about after
       greeting them and before doing business.

       The teaching part carries no fields: which recipe it grants is the subclass, because
       configuration fields on a [Serializable] type become save layout - the same reasoning as the
       eighteen Vixy_Trinket subclasses.

       The choice is invisible unless I already know ModSharp, do not know ModKeen, and hold the
       Tinkering rank its tier requires - Tinker II for all four, one rank above the Tinker I their
       bases sit at. -->
  <conversation ID="YlaHaj">
    <start>
      <choice ID="Vixy_TeachModKeen" Target="Vixy_TaughtModKeen" Ordinal="9700">
        <text>You have the trick of a sharp edge. There is a finer one past it.</text>
        <part Name="Vixy_TeachKeen" />
      </choice>
    </start>
    <node ID="Vixy_TaughtModKeen">
      <text
      >Sharp is where an edge starts. Hold it to the light and grind past the shine - that is keen. Your hands will know when.</text>
      <choice Target="Start">
        <text>Thank you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=.</text>
      </choice>
    </node>
  </conversation>

  <!-- Barathrum the Old teaches legendary. #723.

       One teacher per recipe, so a tinker is a person worth finding rather than one of four
       interchangeable sources. The cost is that killing them removes the recipe from that save.

       Ordinal 9700 puts this below Vixy_ShowMakersMark at 9800 and Vixy_AskName at 10000, and above
       vanilla's water ritual at 980 and [begin trade] at 990 - a thing you ask a tinker about after
       greeting them and before doing business.

       The teaching part carries no fields: which recipe it grants is the subclass, because
       configuration fields on a [Serializable] type become save layout - the same reasoning as the
       eighteen Vixy_Trinket subclasses.

       The choice is invisible unless I already know ModMasterwork, do not know ModLegendary, and hold the
       Tinkering rank its tier requires - Tinker II for all four, one rank above the Tinker I their
       bases sit at. -->
  <conversation ID="Barathrum">
    <start>
      <choice ID="Vixy_TeachModLegendary" Target="Vixy_TaughtModLegendary" Ordinal="9700">
        <text>Master, teach me to make something that outlasts me.</text>
        <part Name="Vixy_TeachLegendary" />
      </choice>
    </start>
    <node ID="Vixy_TaughtModLegendary">
      <text
      >Masterwork is a thing made well. Legendary is a thing made once. The difference is not in the steel, child - it is in refusing to set it down.</text>
      <choice Target="Start">
        <text>Thank you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=.</text>
      </choice>
    </node>
  </conversation>

  <!-- Bep teaches microserrated. #723.

       One teacher per recipe, so a tinker is a person worth finding rather than one of four
